        <p class="article__lead reveal">Zanzibar is where a Tanzania safari exhales — a spice island of turquoise water, white sand and a thousand years of Swahili, Arab and Indian history layered into the coral lanes of Stone Town. It's the perfect place to slow down after the savanna, and a joy for women travelling solo, with friends or as a couple.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Why visit Zanzibar?</h2>
        <p class="reveal">Part of Tanzania but a world of its own, Zanzibar blends culture and beach in a way few places manage. Mornings exploring Stone Town's markets and waterfront, afternoons under a palm with a book, dhows sailing past at sunset — it rounds off a safari beautifully, and stands proudly as a destination in its own right.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Stone Town</h2>
        <p class="reveal">A UNESCO World Heritage Site, Stone Town is a maze of narrow lanes, carved wooden doors, bustling bazaars and call-to-prayer rooftops. Wander with a guide to the Old Fort and the former slave market, browse the spice and fabric stalls, and watch the sun go down over the Forodhani gardens as the street-food grills fire up.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">The beaches</h2>
        <ul class="reveal">
          <li><strong>North coast (Nungwi &amp; Kendwa):</strong> the best swimming beaches, least affected by the tides, with lively sunsets.</li>
          <li><strong>East coast (Paje, Jambiani):</strong> wide white sand, a laid-back vibe and world-class kitesurfing.</li>
          <li><strong>Offshore:</strong> Mnemba Atoll for snorkelling and diving, plus quiet private-island escapes.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="reveal">Things to do</h2>
        <p class="reveal">Beyond the sand: a hands-on spice-farm tour and Swahili cooking class; a traditional dhow cruise to a sandbank; snorkelling the coral gardens of Mnemba; and a morning at Jozani Forest to meet the rare red colobus monkeys found nowhere else on earth.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Best time to visit &amp; what to wear</h2>
        <p class="reveal">The sunniest, driest months are <strong>June–October</strong> and <strong>December–February</strong>; we avoid the long rains of April–May. Zanzibar is predominantly Muslim, so in Stone Town and villages we suggest covering shoulders and knees out of respect — beachwear is perfectly fine at the resorts. See our <a href="best-time-tanzania.html">best time to visit Tanzania</a> guide for the full calendar.</p>

        <h2 class="reveal">Where to stay &amp; how to get there</h2>
        <p class="reveal">We pair a boutique hotel in Stone Town with a beachfront retreat on the coast, matched to the vibe you're after. Most guests fly in by light aircraft straight from the Serengeti, or a short hop from Arusha or Dar es Salaam. Our <a href="zanzibar-womens-retreat.html">Zanzibar Women's Retreat</a> and <a href="serengeti-and-zanzibar.html">Serengeti &amp; Zanzibar</a> journeys both make the most of the island.</p>

